Every ramfs and tmpfs mount shares one limit (ramfs.limit, half of RAM by default) and takes tmpfs's
size= for one of its own; a new page past either is ENOSPC, written or read in from a hole (ramfs
keeps a zeroed page for a hole it reads, so reading a sparse file filled memory too). Without
CONFIG_SHMEM (it needs an MMU) tmpfs is ramfs, which ignores size= and has no limit at all: files in
/tmp, an overlay's upper layer or the root could take the whole 4 GiB, and there is no swap and
nothing the OOM killer could free. Pages are counted in i_blocks (du and stat show them), brought to
the page cache's nrpages when a file is written, truncated or evicted; df shows a mount's own use
and the room left.

